        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.444,
            0.881,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "**Q.8. Recall the sequence of Powers of 2 from Chapter 1, Table 1. Why is the Collatz conjecture correct for all the starting numbers in this sequence?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.48,
            0.882,
            0.534
          ],
          "content": "Ans. when we divide $2^{8} = 2x2x2x2x2x2x2x2$ by 2 it become $2 ^ { 7 }$ and every time you divide by 2 the same will continue happening, until you are left with 2 which when divided by 2 will leave 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.55,
            0.737,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "**Q.9. Check if the Collatz Conjecture holds for the starting number 100.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.568,
            0.88,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": "Ans. 100, 50, 25, 76, 38, 19, 58, 29, 88, 44, 22, 11, 34, 17, 52, 26, 13, 40, 20, 10, 5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.621,
            0.88,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": "**Q.10 Starting with 0, players alternate adding numbers between 1 and 3. The first person to reach 22 wins. What is the winning strategy now?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.658,
            0.515,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "Ans. Winning strategy is to be the first player."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
